                  <provenance><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_435">Oxford, Oxfordshire. Merton College.
                        </orgName><p>‘Made in England, at the College by 1372: UO48. 79. Apparently
                        lost to the College for a time before being reacquired by Richard Fitzjames.
                        In the centre of f. iii a shield-shaped piece of parchment (55 × 40 mm) has
                        been hacked out. on f. ivv an inscription of four lines, possibly Fitzjames’
                        ex dono, has been thoroughly erased. Just below this is the impression of
                        the usual Fitzjames label, with his coat of arms visible under ultra-violet
                        light, and the marks of nail-hols, therefore originally on the back cover,
                        then pasted to this leaf, since stripped off and lost.’ (Thomson, Merton,
                        225)It is not clear why Fitzjames’s marks should have been thus
                        obliterated.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Richard Fitzjames (d. 1522), fellow and
                     warden of Merton, bishop of Rochester, Cicester, and London</provenance>
               </history>
